Sval: a WebGL context the application loses on purpose is not warned about

WEBGL_lose_context.loseContext() logs "WebGL context was lost." to the
console like any other loss. Troubleshoot.sys.mjs probes the GPU with a
WebGL 1 and a WebGL 2 context and loses each to free it, so about:support,
the feedback form and Report Broken Site each log the warning twice. The
warning is for the authors of web pages, which still get it, and a loss the
application did not ask for still warns. The caller decides: Troubleshoot's
canvas is in a DOMParser document, which DOMParser::Constructor gives a null
principal when its caller is the system principal.

diff --git a/dom/canvas/ClientWebGLContext.cpp b/dom/canvas/ClientWebGLContext.cpp
index 131a739bde..6da6f51f44 100644
--- a/dom/canvas/ClientWebGLContext.cpp
+++ b/dom/canvas/ClientWebGLContext.cpp
@@ -291,7 +291,15 @@ void ClientWebGLContext::EmulateLoseContext() const {
 
 void ClientWebGLContext::OnContextLoss(
     const webgl::ContextLossReason reason) const {
-  JsWarning("WebGL context was lost.");
+  // The warning is for the authors of web pages. The application's own code
+  // calls WEBGL_lose_context.loseContext() to free a context it is done with,
+  // often in a document DOMParser gave a null principal, so the caller is
+  // what tells them apart. A manual loss arrives here inside that call.
+  const bool lostByChrome = reason == webgl::ContextLossReason::Manual &&
+                            nsContentUtils::ThreadsafeIsCallerChrome();
+  if (!lostByChrome) {
+    JsWarning("WebGL context was lost.");
+  }
 
   RefPtr<webgl::NotLostData> notLost(mNotLost);
   if (notLost) {
